<!-- SOC-AI - rule 100659 (level 12). Domain: threat_hunting,linux,mitre_gaps,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="threat_hunting,linux,mitre_gaps,">

  <!-- R2b T1059.004 / T1571 - a listener bind shell that 100651 misses.
       100651 fires on nc/ncat WITH an exec option (-e/-c), covering the classic
       `nc -lvp 4444 -e /bin/bash`. But a bind shell can listen WITHOUT an exec
       option (payload wires the socket to a shell itself, a named-pipe backpipe,
       or socat LISTEN...EXEC), and none of those carry -e/-c on the command
       line. Measured 2026-07-29: a `nc -l 4444` bind shell on jellyfin fired
       nothing.

       Signals on a LISTEN option: an nc/ncat option bundle containing `l`
       (-l, -lvp, -nvlp...) or the socat `LISTEN` token. socat is matched only
       here (100651 does not know socat).

       Overlap with 100651 is deliberate and harmless: a `nc -lvp -e` event
       matches both, Wazuh fires one, and either is a level-12 shell alert - no
       detection is masked (the README trap is about LOSING a detection, not
       about two equivalent alerts). The pattern is a bare token like 100651's,
       NOT anchored: in production the event is multiline (SYSCALL+EXECVE) and a
       `^...*` anchor would never reach the EXECVE args.

       Scope: on these servers no legitimate service runs as an interactive
       nc/socat listener, so a listener is anomalous by itself - level 12. If a
       real service ever does this, whitelist the exact command. -->

  <rule id="100659" level="12">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">/(nc|ncat|netcat|socat)(\.\w+)?$</field>
    <regex type="pcre2">a\d+=\"-[a-z]*l[a-z]*\"|LISTEN</regex>
    <description>Probable bind shell: netcat/socat listening socket without exec flag</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
      <id>T1571</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
